Detector design details:

```python
SET_BASED_PATTERNS = [
  r"contained in J\s*[-^]?\s*\(?\s*I\+",   # "contained in J-(I+)"
  r"union of J\s*[-^]?\s*\(?q",             # "union of J^-(q) over all q"
  r"J\s*[-^]\s*\(I\+\)",                    # any J-(I+) reference
]
```
Careful: AF-WCC-VAC-GEN's repaired text mentions "The set-based reading (gamma contained in the union of J^-(q) over all q in I+) is strictly stronger; it is registered as variant SET..." — it *mentions* the set-based reading in order to disown it. So a naive regex would flag the repaired class. Need context-aware detection: set-based reading is a *violation* only if it appears in the conclusion's *assertive* clause, not inside a disowning/variant-registration sentence.

How to distinguish mechanically? Use negation/registration markers within the same sentence:
- Disowning markers: "set-based", "variant", "registered", "strictly stronger", "not a", "never be interchanged", "must never".
- Assertive set-based: "contained in J-(I+)" without any of the disowning markers in the sentence.

Approach: split conclusion text into sentences (split on ". " and "; "), classify each sentence:
- `assertive_setbased(s)`: matches set-based regex AND no disown marker AND no "variant"/"reading" noun.
- For current SCALAR-SPH: sentence "For generic data in the class, the MGHD admits I+ and every future-inextendible causal geodesic contained in J-(I+) is complete; equivalently, the singularities that form are hidden behind an event horizon and no singularity is visible from I+." → no disown markers → assertive. Flag.
- For VAC-GEN: "The set-based reading (gamma contained in the union of J^-(q) over all q in I+) is strictly stronger; it is registered as variant `SET` of this class..." → has "set-based"/"variant"/"registered"/"strictly stronger" → not assertive. Good.

Similarly equivalence detector:
- `ASSERTED_EQUIV_RE = r"\bequivalently\b|\bis equivalent to\b|\bequivalence\b"` but exclude disclaimers: "not an asserted equivalence", "must never be interchanged", "not equivalent".
- Sentence-level: flag if asserted-equivalence regex matches and no disclaim marker ("not", "never", "no " before? too broad). Use explicit disclaimer regex: `r"not an asserted equivalence|not equivalent|never.{0,30}interchang|does not assert|no claim of equivalence"`.
- Current SCALAR-SPH: "equivalently, the singularities that form are hidden..." → flag.
- VAC-GEN: "This is a definition, not an asserted equivalence with any other formulation (review HF-06)." → disclaimer → not flagged. Good.
- C2/C0: no "equival" → not flagged.

